Parameter markers represent data values, not table names, column names, or SQL keywords such as `ASC` and `DESC`; see the PDO parameter-marker limitations. Keep these structural choices fixed in code where possible. When users need to select among them, map their selection to a small set of application-owned values allowed for that query and reject anything else. In this example, `tableName` can only hold one of two fixed values, so it can be appended to the query:

```java
String tableName;
switch(PARAM) {
  case "room":
    tableName = "roomTable";
    break;
  case "floor":
    tableName = "floorTable";
    break;
  default:
    throw new InputValidationException("unexpected table name");
}
```

Map a requested sort direction the same way, to the literal `ASC` or `DESC`, rather than appending the supplied text.

